==== NAME
umount.pdxfs — detach a mounted PdxFS v1 volume.

==== SYNOPSIS
    umount.pdxfs [--lazy] [--force] [--verbose] [--dry-run] <mount-id | mount-point>

==== DESCRIPTION
`umount.pdxfs` resolves a `<mount-id | mount-point>` target to a
`_mount_table[]` slot, checks the volume's open-handle count, drives a
`sys_umount` attempt plus a clean-checkpoint/re-sign/row-remove
sequence, and prints a `PdxFsUnmountRecord@0.1` line describing the
outcome. It is the reversal of `mount.pdxfs` (and, transitively, of
`mkfs.pdxfs`) — see `[[cref: mkfs.pdxfs#NAME]]` and
`[[cref: mount.pdxfs#NAME]]`.

A `<mount-id>` is the u64 value a prior `PdxFsMountRecord@0.1`'s
`mount_id` field carried (confirmed to equal the real `_mount_table[]`
slot index `sys_mount` itself returns — there is no separate persistent
allocator). A `<mount-point>` is a VFS path; at this release, resolving
a path to a mount_id always fails (`MOUNT_NOT_FOUND`) — the real
per-slot mount-table layout carries no path-comparable field. Pass the
numeric `<mount-id>` instead until this closes; see
`[[cref: LIMITATIONS]]`.

==== FLAGS
    --lazy       Do not refuse an unmount over open handles; defer
                 instead. At this release the deferral is emitted
                 (result_code: DEFERRED) but no completion record ever
                 follows — see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].
    --force      Bypass the open-handle refusal by revoking handles,
                 subject to an elevation check for protected mount
                 points. At this release the elevation check is
                 fail-closed (no broker-endpoint capability exists for
                 this tool to request elevation with) — every --force
                 invocation over open handles returns
                 result_code: ELEVATION_STUB. See [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].
    --verbose    Parsed, not yet consumed by any output path.
    --dry-run    Print the PdxFsUnmountRecord@0.1 line that would be
                 emitted, without unmounting anything. Always exits 0.

==== EXIT CODES
     0   OK — the target unmounted (or a --dry-run preview / --lazy
         schedule; see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]] for what "unmounted" means
         at this release's stub coverage).
     2   Missing the required <mount-id | mount-point> positional.
     3   MOUNT_NOT_FOUND — the target did not resolve to a mount-table
         slot.
     5   IN_USE — open handles block the unmount and neither --force
         nor --lazy was given.
     6   KERNEL_ERROR — sys_umount returned a genuine (non-stub) error.
     7   ELEVATION_STUB — --force was given but the fail-closed
         elevation check denied it.
     8   SIG_KEY_LOCKED — the superblock could not be re-signed.
         Unreachable at this release; see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

==== RECORD FORMAT
Every non-dry-run invocation prints one deferral header line (semantic-
pipe binding is not yet live; see [[cref: LIMITATIONS]]) followed by:

    PdxFsUnmountRecord@0.1 { target: <t>, mount_id: <id>,
      result_code: <OK|IN_USE|KERNEL_ERROR|MOUNT_NOT_FOUND|DEFERRED|
                     ELEVATION_STUB|SIG_KEY_LOCKED>,
      handle_count: <n>, stub_mask: <bits> }

`stub_mask` documents exactly which of `umount.pdxfs`'s own six
teardown substeps ran for real vs. stubbed for this invocation — see
`[[cref: LIMITATIONS]]` for the bit table.

==== LIMITATIONS
This release's stub coverage, spelled out so a caller does not mistake
absence for a bug:

  - **`<mount-point>` path resolution** always fails. Pass the numeric
    `<mount-id>` instead.
  - **Open-handle counting** always reports 0 — no kernel op exists to
    query it yet, and no way exists to derive which capability a
    resolved mount_id even corresponds to. The `IN_USE` refusal is real
    logic that never fires through the real query path.
  - **`sys_umount` itself** is dispatched but the kernel's own dispatch
    table has never been wired to the (otherwise complete) syscall
    body — every real attempt observes a stubbed `-ENOSYS`, recorded in
    `stub_mask` bit 0x1, and the tool proceeds as if it had succeeded.
  - **Cache flush, transaction drain, and CLEAN checkpoint** (bits
    0x2, 0x4) have no kernel primitive at all.
  - **Superblock re-sign** (bit 0x8) runs a REAL cryptographic sign
    call, but against a placeholder buffer, not the genuine on-disk
    superblock — no block-device read primitive exists for this tool.
  - **Mount-table row removal** (bit 0x10) has no kernel op.
  - **`--lazy`'s completion record** never fires — no kernel primitive
    notifies a process when a volume's last handle closes.
  - **`--force`'s elevation check** always denies — no broker-endpoint
    capability has been granted to this tool.
  - **`SIG_KEY_LOCKED`** cannot occur at this release — the signing
    seed used is a real, non-null, all-zero buffer, not an unavailable
    one, and the signing intrinsic only fails on a null seed.

See `[[cref: umount.pdxfs#CHANGELOG]]` (this repo's `CHANGELOG.md`) for
the full, itemised list with the exact source file and issue number
each limitation traces to.

